title: $:/plugins/tiddlywiki/bibtex/readme

The BibTeX plugin provides a deserializer that can convert bibliographic entries in `.bib` files into individual tiddlers.

You can use it in the browser by dragging and dropping a `.bib` file into the TiddlyWiki window. Under Node.js, use the `--load` command to load a `.bib` file.

The conversion is as follows:

* `title` comes from citationKey
* `bibtex-entry-type` comes from entryType
* all `entryTags` are assigned to fields with the prefix `bibtex-`
* `@string` macros are expanded, ignoring case. An undefined macro keeps its name

A bad entry does not stop the import. The other entries are imported, and the tiddler "BibTeX import error" lists the line and problem of each bad one.

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/config/BibTeX/Standard" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="no"> Follow BibTeX's own rules and convert field values to plain text</$checkbox>

This option is off by default, and applies to later imports only. When it is on:

* braces are removed, so `The {TeX}book` becomes `The TeXbook`
* accents and special letters are decoded, so `M{\"u}ller` becomes `Müller` and `Stra{\ss}e` becomes `Straße`
* `\&`, `\%`, `\_`, `\#` and `\$` become the plain character
* runs of spaces and line breaks become one space
* unknown commands and math between `$` signs are kept as written
* `url`, `doi` and `eprint` are kept as written
* `bibtex-entry-type` and the title are cleaned up, so `@BOOK` gives `book`
* braces count as they do in BibTeX, so a quote inside braces belongs to the value in `"x {"}y"`, and `\}` closes a value in `{x\}}`

The plugin adds a "BibTeX" tab to the search results, which searches every field of the imported entries except `created`, `modified`, `creator` and `modifier`. The tab appears whenever the plugin is installed, even if the wiki has no entries.

The `citeAPA` template cites an entry in running text, as a link to its tiddler with the title as tooltip: `{{Dalgaard2001||citeAPA}}` shows `(Dalgaard, 2001)`.

! Other uses

An entry is a tiddler with `bibtex-` fields, and any entry type and field name can be imported, so a `.bib` file can hold more than publications:

|!Use |!Entry type |!Useful fields |
|Bookmarks and read-later lists |`@online` |`url`, `urldate`, `keywords` |
|Software and datasets |`@software`, `@dataset` |`version`, `url`, `doi` |
|Standards, RFCs and datasheets |`@manual`, `@techreport` |`number`, `institution`, `url` |
|Personal library of books, films and music |`@book`, `@movie`, `@audio` |`publisher`, `year`, `keywords` |
|Patents and legal sources |`@patent`, `@legislation` |`number`, `date` |
|Letters and archive material |`@letter`, `@misc` |`author`, `date`, `location` |
|Course reading lists |any |`keywords`, `note` |
|Your own record types, such as recipes or contacts |`@recipe`, `@contact` |any field name |

The text of an imported entry is empty, so it has room for your own notes. Importing the same citation key again replaces the tiddler, including those notes.

Without `author` and `year`, `citeAPA` shows the title and `n.d.`

! Library

The BibTeX plugin is based on the library [[bibtexParseJs by Henrik Muehe and Mikola Lysenko|https://github.com/ORCID/bibtexParseJs]]. Its copy in this plugin adds support for `@string` macros, parentheses around entries, entries without fields, missing commas and `@` signs in text between entries, reports bad entries instead of failing, and has an option for BibTeX's own rules, so it differs from the original.

See also: [[BibTeXing by Oren Patashnik|https://mirrors.ctan.org/biblio/bibtex/base/btxdoc.pdf]] and [[the BibTeX format at bibtex.org|https://www.bibtex.org/Format/]].
